2	// Reads a static-site repo's `.github/site.config` and emits its keys as step
3	// outputs. Vendored into each site repo's own .github/actions/; dependency-free
4	// (node: built-ins only) so it runs on a bare runner with no npm ci.
5	//
6	// REQUIRED AND FULLY EXPLICIT — five keys, no defaults. A default that "happens
7	// to match" a repo's layout silently publishes the wrong tree the day the layout
8	// or the default changes, and the published set is exactly the thing that must
9	// never be guessed:
10	//
11	//   publish_root    the directory the published site is rooted at ("." = the
12	//                   repo root). Every publish path is relative to it, and it is
13	//                   what becomes the site's "/".
14	//   publish_paths   the explicit publish set — the files and directories under
15	//                   publish_root that are copied into the artifact, and nothing
16	//                   else. Space-separated, relative to publish_root.
17	//   version_files   the files carrying the version, first one the source of
18	//                   truth. Space-separated.
19	//   build_command   the command that produces whatever publish_paths names
20	//                   ("" = nothing to build, stated).
21	//   test_command    the repo's CI gate ("" = no tests, stated).
22	//
23	// A missing file, a missing key, an unknown (typo'd) key and an empty required
24	// path key are all hard failures — a pipeline that "worked" because a typo fell
25	// back to a default is the failure mode this shape exists to prevent.
26	//
27	// One key is OPTIONAL, and deliberately so:
28	//
29	//   build_vars      space-separated names of repo VARIABLES the build may read,
30	//                   exported into build_command's environment (absent = none).
31	//
32	// It is optional because the explicitness rule above exists to stop a default
33	// from silently publishing the WRONG TREE — and an absent build_vars cannot do
34	// that. Absent means the build sees no variables, which is what every site did
35	// before the key existed, so an untouched config keeps its exact meaning. What
36	// is NOT tolerated is a declared name with no value behind it: that is the
37	// silently half-configured build — a blank analytics token, an empty base URL —
38	// baked into a published page, so a declared-but-unset variable fails the run.
39	//
40	// VARIABLES, NEVER SECRETS. The exporter beside this file is handed
41	// `toJSON(vars)`, a context that structurally cannot carry a secret. A value
42	// that must not appear in a published artifact has no business reaching the
43	// step whose output IS that artifact, so the boundary is enforced by which
44	// context the workflow passes, not by a convention someone has to remember.
